{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"body":"Pre-sweep against origin/main `0886e96f03a` — description amended before spending a gate slot.\n\n**Two AC defects found and fixed; no scope change.**\n\n1. **AC1 could pass with half the work undone.** It grepped case-sensitively for `deprecated alias`. `docs/ticket-model.md:300` matches, but `src/rebar/_commands/composer_edit.py:189` reads \"a **DEPRECATED** alias\" in caps and therefore never matched. The criterion was already satisfied on that file before any edit. AC1 is now `grep -in`, and gains a second clause asserting the stale \"intercepted in edit_core\" claim is gone — that sentence is the substantive error in the comment, and no spelling of the first grep detects it.\n\n2. **AC2's exempt fixture does not exist.** The plan said to leave `:472` alone as a fixture name. There is no `[operator-attested]` at `:472`. The file holds exactly 4 occurrences — `:112`, `:121`, `:1160`, `:1185` — and the Approach rewrites all 4, so `grep -c` = 0 is reachable. `[non-codebase]` is 1 today (`:317`), so the post-state is exactly 5. AC2 now states both numbers rather than `>= 5`.\n\n**Every citation had drifted.** Re-measured and corrected in the description's new table: `ticket-model.md` 288→300, `composer_edit.py` 200-203→187-190, `plan-review-gate.md` 113/122-123/1158/1183→112/121/1160/1185, `migrations.md` 99/256/260/264/293→119/276/280/284/313. Two citations named the wrong file: the removal is enforced at `composer_edit.py:282` (not `:301-302`) with the CLI refusal at `:392-394`/`:401` (not `:422-426`), and the MCP tag params live in `_mcp_tool_specs.py:63-65` (not `_mcp_writes.py:361-380`, which contains no such symbols).\n\n**The premise checks out.** `src/rebar/_lib_mutations.py:151-159` already documents the correct contract — the `tags=` set-alias \"was removed pre-1.0 — DE7; it is now rejected as an unknown field\" — and pops only `add_tags`/`remove_tags`/`set_tags`, so `tags` reaches `edit_core`'s unknown-field rejection. `edit_core` (`:262-270`) takes `tag_add`/`tag_remove`/`tag_set` and performs no `tags` interception, which is why `composer_edit.py:190`'s claim is stale. This story aligns two prose sites with a source of truth that is already right; it changes no behaviour.\n\nAlso added: the Testing section now records that none of the pinned modules carries an `integration`/`external` marker, so `make verify` genuinely executes them."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793473091657940001,"uuid":"d8e4327a-446e-4864-96bc-7a9ef9ec55d9"}